<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Croatian Peasant Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Croatian Peasant Party is a Croatian political party founded in 1904 by the brothers Antun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a>. Between the world wars it was the main voice of Croatian opposition to Serbian-dominated centralism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and after Stjepan Radić was shot in parliament in 1928 it was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a>. In 1939 Maček's agreement with the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic">Dragiša Cvetković</a> created the autonomous Banovina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 22 December 1904 as the Croatian People's Peasant Party (HPSS), in the Kingdom of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> within the Hungarian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. The Radić brothers distrusted the traditional parties and built a grassroots network around national unity and agrarianism, and they believed Croatian statehood could be achieved within a monarchy reformed into three equal parts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The party mattered little before 1918, but its antimilitarism and pacifism won wide support in the last year of the war, and universal manhood suffrage gave the vote to the peasants, then 80% of the population of Croatia-Slavonia.</p><p>In the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> it became the only significant party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. It took the name Croatian Republican Peasant Party in 1920 and called for a neutral peasant republic. After a campaign of arrests before the 1925 election, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> accepted the monarchy in return for his release and a place in a coalition led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-radical-party">People's Radical Party</a>, and the party became the Croatian Peasant Party (HSS).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The HSS left the government in January 1927 and in November formed the Peasant-Democratic Coalition with the Independent Democratic Party, the most popular party among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. On 20 June 1928 the Radical deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punisa-racic">Puniša Račić</a> shot several HSS members in parliament, killing two; Radić died of his wounds on 8 August. After King Alexander declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in 1929 the party was banned. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a> it set up a paramilitary force, the Croatian Peasant Defence, in 1936, and as part of the United Opposition won most votes in 1938 but only 67 of 373 seats. In 1938 and 1939 Maček also corresponded with the Italian foreign minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano">Galeazzo Ciano</a>, but declined an Italian offer to finance an uprising.</p><p>The agreement of 26 August 1939 made Maček deputy prime minister, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> denounced him as a traitor. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> some members joined the Ustaše and others the Partisans, though most supporters stayed passive. After the communist victory the party was outlawed; Maček led it in exile until his death in 1964 and Juraj Krnjević until 1988. It was restored in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> during the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and took part in coalition governments from 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Croatian Peasant Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Croatian Peasant Party</b> leader Stjepan Radić was shot in the Belgrade parliament in 1928, King Alexander proclaimed a royal dictatorship in January 1929, and the Ustaša was created in principle that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Croatian Peasant Party</b> of Stjepan Radić demanded autonomy or a federal republic; Radić was imprisoned, then briefly joined the government in 1925.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>With war looming, the regent Prince Paul sought agreement with the <b>Croatian Peasant Party</b>, and the Cvetković-Maček Agreement of 26 August 1939 merged the Sava and Littoral banovinas with parts of Bosnia, Herzegovina, Srem and the Dubrovnik area into one Banovina of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/banovina-of-croatia">Banovina of Croatia</a></li></ul></section>
